<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<odoo noupdate="1">

    <!-- The email button/footer colour, seeded on the default company.

         email_secondary_color lives in `mail`, not `base` (declared at
         addons/mail/models/res_company.py:33), which is why this seed belongs to
         this overlay and not to the base addon that depends on `web` alone.

         Reaches, with zero source edits:
           * mail_notification_layout  (mail_templates_email_layouts.xml:105)
           * mail_notification_light   (:179)
           * digest_mail_layout        (digest_data.xml:14)
         and does NOT reach the sites that hardcode their colour inline
         (auth_signup, im_livechat, website_slides, hr_expense) — those are
         replaced in markup by their own overlays.

         base.main_company is updated, not created: it is a real xmlid declared
         in base/data/res_company_data.xml. Writing a bare <record> without the
         base. prefix would create a second company named CASTADOO, which is the
         failure this comment exists to prevent.

         KNOWN SIDE EFFECT, stated rather than hidden: this writes on first
         install, so a database whose operator had already chosen a different
         button colour will see it replaced. The value is one field on the
         default company, and noupdate="1" keeps it from being re-applied on
         every later module update. The deployment checklist states the value so
         an operator can restore their own. Seeding it is still the right trade:
         without it every notification email ships Odoo purple (#875A7B) on a
         user-facing surface.

         Also note the field is computed from `secondary_color`, so an operator
         who later edits that field re-derives this one back to the stock
         #875A7B. That is upstream behaviour and not preventable here. -->

    <record id="base.main_company" model="res.company">
        <field name="email_secondary_color">#303188</field>
    </record>

</odoo>
